<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhins of Galloway</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhinns of Galloway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhins of Galloway is a hammer-headed peninsula in the far south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, joined to the mainland by a narrow isthmus between Loch Ryan and Luce Bay. Its southern tip, the Mull of Galloway, is Scotland's most southerly point, and Stranraer and Portpatrick lie on it. The Novantarum Promontorium of the geographer Ptolemy is usually identified with it, and the church at Kirkmadrine holds some of Scotland's earliest Christian stones.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhins-of-galloway.jpg" alt="Rhins of Galloway" width="320" height="592" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SFC9394 · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhins_of_Galloway_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The peninsula ends in the north at Corsewall and Milleur Points, measures 28 miles (45 kilometres) from end to end and takes its name from the Gaelic rinn, point. Its west coast, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-channel">North Channel</a>, has steep cliffs, while the calmer east coast has sandy beaches. Exposed to Atlantic westerlies, it receives around 1,000 millimetres of rain a year and is mainly dairy and beef farmland, and the North Atlantic drift keeps frosts rare enough for palms to grow at the Logan Botanic Garden. In ancient times boats were dragged across the isthmus between the East and West Tarbets to avoid the currents off the Mull.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Britons are recalled in the farm name Drumbreddan, the ridge of the Britons; their chieftains lived in hill-forts such as Dunman and in brochs such as Doon Castle at Ardwell Point. The Novantae, a Celtic tribe, were based in the area. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 81, a road was built west from Dalswinton to Stranraer, leading some to argue that any attack he made on Ireland would have started here. Crofting, salt pans and kelp harvesting long sustained a sparse population.</p><p>Sea links to Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> later carried farm produce out, and a railway was laid to Portpatrick for the mail boat to Donaghadee. As ships grew, the route moved in 1849 to Stranraer Harbour in Loch Ryan, from where ferries still sail to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> and Larne. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the area was an important station for anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a> work, with flying boats at RAF Station Wig Bay and RAF Station Stranraer, and Luce Bay was a bombing range.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Rhins are a patchwork of farms, increasingly turning to tourism. The Mull of Galloway is a Site of Special Scientific Interest and an RSPB nature reserve where razorbills, guillemots and puffins nest. The Southern Upland Way begins at Portpatrick, and Lochnaw Castle near Leswalt is the ancestral seat of Clan Agnew.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhins of Galloway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ptolemy lists the Novantae in south-western Scotland, giving their name to a peninsula, the Novantarum Promontorium, usually identified with the <b>Rhins of Galloway</b>, and naming two towns, Locopibia and Rerigonium.</blockquote><a href="/state/novantae">Novantae</a></li></ul></section>
